1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:02,597
Now, running scripts manually as we did here

2
00:00:02,597 --> 00:00:05,339
is one thing you may wanna do for irregular

3
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:08,177
tasks, tasks that just need to be executed

4
00:00:08,193 --> 00:00:10,534
from time to time. But of course, often you

5
00:00:10,598 --> 00:00:12,907
also have recurring tasks and a backup, as

6
00:00:12,923 --> 00:00:15,120
mentioned, is a great example for a

7
00:00:15,184 --> 00:00:16,130
recurring task.

8
00:00:16,948 --> 00:00:19,577
Therefore, we also may wanna run our script

9
00:00:19,577 --> 00:00:22,479
on a schedule using a feature called cron.

10
00:00:22,944 --> 00:00:25,558
And cron is simply a feature of Linux

11
00:00:25,606 --> 00:00:29,853
systems, a pre-installed process or tool,

12
00:00:29,869 --> 00:00:30,386
you could say,

13
00:00:31,082 --> 00:00:35,236
that allows you to run scripts, files, tasks

14
00:00:35,753 --> 00:00:38,082
on a schedule. Now, first of all, we can

15
00:00:38,098 --> 00:00:40,946
check whether cron is enabled on our system

16
00:00:40,962 --> 00:00:41,873
with this command.

17
00:00:44,417 --> 00:00:46,616
And you see it is active for me here. It

18
00:00:46,616 --> 00:00:47,521
should be for you too.

19
00:00:49,025 --> 00:00:52,032
If it's not active, this is how you can make

20
00:00:52,032 --> 00:00:54,129
sure you got it installed and how you can

21
00:00:54,129 --> 00:00:54,714
enable it.

22
00:00:55,845 --> 00:00:58,100
And then we could run our backup script on a

23
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:00,597
schedule every day at 9:00 AM, for example.

24
00:01:01,483 --> 00:01:03,512
Now, to do that, you can run a command

25
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:04,317
called crontab,

26
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:06,774
which allows you to manage your cron

27
00:01:07,049 --> 00:01:09,258
entries, your schedules, so to say.

28
00:01:09,952 --> 00:01:12,710
However, we don't wanna run crontab like

29
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:14,549
this, but instead with sudo.

30
00:01:16,113 --> 00:01:18,615
So here I'll run sudo crontab -e,

31
00:01:20,229 --> 00:01:22,457
and this shows me my crontab, and currently,

32
00:01:22,822 --> 00:01:25,650
It's empty because I haven't created any

33
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:26,533
schedules before.

34
00:01:27,176 --> 00:01:29,023
So therefore, since this is the first time

35
00:01:29,023 --> 00:01:30,244
I'm trying to edit this file,

36
00:01:30,919 --> 00:01:32,847
I have to choose which editor I wanna use

37
00:01:32,847 --> 00:01:35,867
and I'll use nano, so number 1 or nothing

38
00:01:35,996 --> 00:01:38,647
since that is the default and hit Enter and

39
00:01:38,647 --> 00:01:41,619
that opens up a simple file that contains my

40
00:01:41,715 --> 00:01:42,310
schedules.

41
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:45,796
Now cron schedules have their very own

42
00:01:45,957 --> 00:01:49,105
notation and attached you find a link to

43
00:01:49,636 --> 00:01:52,702
more details about how you can configure

44
00:01:52,702 --> 00:01:54,886
cron tasks. And of course, your favorite AI

45
00:01:55,015 --> 00:01:56,909
agent can also help you with that, or

46
00:01:57,118 --> 00:01:57,857
ChatGPT.

47
00:01:58,868 --> 00:02:00,137
Here in my case,

48
00:02:00,795 --> 00:02:01,662
I already

49
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:02,706
have

50
00:02:03,958 --> 00:02:05,772
some code which I want to paste in, so I'll

51
00:02:05,772 --> 00:02:06,591
copy that,

52
00:02:07,555 --> 00:02:08,711
paste it into that file,

53
00:02:09,385 --> 00:02:12,002
and I now also want to edit this here. Now

54
00:02:12,372 --> 00:02:14,588
this just says every day at 9 AM

55
00:02:15,198 --> 00:02:15,877
system time,

56
00:02:16,541 --> 00:02:19,598
and my user is called Max. My script, of

57
00:02:19,614 --> 00:02:21,814
course, is directly stored in a scripts

58
00:02:21,879 --> 00:02:22,218
folder,

59
00:02:23,815 --> 00:02:25,233
and that is then the script name.

60
00:02:25,974 --> 00:02:29,826
And I then also write my logs to a dedicated

61
00:02:29,923 --> 00:02:33,050
log file. This forwards my output from the

62
00:02:33,050 --> 00:02:34,177
script to that log file.

63
00:02:35,498 --> 00:02:36,174
And that is all

64
00:02:36,722 --> 00:02:38,897
it should take to run this script on a

65
00:02:38,897 --> 00:02:41,555
schedule. Of course, you must make sure that

66
00:02:41,555 --> 00:02:43,860
the script works. That's why I ran it

67
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:46,192
manually in the previous lectures.

68
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:50,873
With that, I can exit out of this file, save

69
00:02:50,873 --> 00:02:53,437
it so that this new entry is saved,

70
00:02:53,982 --> 00:02:56,884
and that is all it should take. You can now

71
00:02:57,188 --> 00:02:59,561
run sudo crontab -l

72
00:03:00,186 --> 00:03:03,119
to see basically the content of that file

73
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:05,267
and to see that this was saved and is

74
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:06,678
registered. So

75
00:03:07,223 --> 00:03:08,425
-l simply lists it,

76
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:11,968
-e edits that file. That is what we did. You

77
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:16,665
can also run the timedatectl command to see

78
00:03:16,745 --> 00:03:20,720
your local time because this runs after your

79
00:03:21,153 --> 00:03:24,696
local time on the VPS. So you'll see for me,

80
00:03:25,321 --> 00:03:29,954
I'm recording this at 6:00 AM local time on

81
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:33,496
the VPS. So it's still almost 3 hours for me

82
00:03:33,496 --> 00:03:34,282
for this to run.

83
00:03:34,971 --> 00:03:36,606
So in case you're waiting for it to run,

84
00:03:36,718 --> 00:03:39,636
don't use your local time of where you are,

85
00:03:40,085 --> 00:03:42,618
check the local time of your VPS instead.

86
00:03:42,794 --> 00:03:44,978
That's important. And then you can check

87
00:03:45,042 --> 00:03:48,162
whether it executed after your local time

88
00:03:48,757 --> 00:03:51,813
made it past the script execution time, the

89
00:03:51,877 --> 00:03:54,498
cron time you configured. And that is all

90
00:03:54,707 --> 00:03:58,293
you need to do to run any script, any

91
00:03:58,631 --> 00:04:01,799
program, any command of your choice on a

92
00:04:01,799 --> 00:04:04,919
schedule. It's as simple as that on a Linux

93
00:04:04,919 --> 00:04:05,369
VPS.
